#! Temporal Displacement Runtime Implementation
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Implements the TemporalDisplacement abstraction for this program.
##
## HumanModel:
##   This program creates, validates, and executes Observer state
##   transitions according to the TemporalDisplacement contract.
!#

#! Observer Initial State Validation
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Validates that the Observer state at program initialization
##   satisfies the required Observer State Model.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   Invalid initial state prevents displacement execution.
!#

#! Speed of light in vacuum - Physical Constant
## Symbol: c
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A physical constant representing the invariant speed at which
##   electromagnetic radiation propagates through a vacuum.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The speed of light in vacuum is represented as a fixed physical
##   constant used as a fundamental relationship between distance and
##   time measurements.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The speed of light in vacuum is a constant value exactly equal to
##   299,792,458 meters per second.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   MeasurementType:
##     Velocity
##
##   ReferenceSystem:
##     International System of Units (SI)
##
##   ReferenceMedium:
##     Vacuum
##
##   DistanceUnit:
##     Meter
##
##   TimeUnit:
##     Second
##
##   Value:
##     299,792,458 meters per second
##
##   Definition:
##     The distance traveled by light in vacuum during one second.
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on independently observable and
##     measurable physical evidence concerning the propagation of
##     electromagnetic radiation through vacuum and its relationship
##     between distance and elapsed time.
##
##   ReferenceIntegrity:
##     The reference system, distance unit, time unit, and physical
##     conditions used to determine or realize the speed of light
##     must be established independently of the value being verified.
##
##   MeasurementIntegrity:
##     Evidence used to determine or realize the speed of light must
##     not be altered by the entity or process whose measurement is
##     being verified.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine or realize the speed of light
##     must be explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine the speed of light from the available evidence,
##     reference definitions, and assumptions.
##
##   MeasurementConfidence:
##     When the physical realization or independent measurement of the
##     speed of light cannot be established directly, the verification
##     process may provide a probability representing the estimated
##     likelihood that the measured or realized value corresponds to
##     the defined physical constant.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the measurement or physical realization
##     of the speed of light must be explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeMeasurements:
##     Physically plausible alternative interpretations of the
##     available electromagnetic, spatial, or temporal evidence must
##     be identified and considered where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableMeasurements:
##     If two or more physically distinct measurement conditions
##     produce no physically observable distinction within the
##     available evidence, verification must not represent the value
##     as certain solely because one interpretation is preferred by
##     the inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish the measurement or
##     physical realization of the speed of light with sufficient
##     justification, verification must report the unresolved
##     measurement rather than represent the value as established.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

#! Meter - Unit of Length
## Symbol: m
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A base unit of length measurement defined as the length of the
##   path traveled by light in vacuum during the time interval of
##   1/299,792,458 of a second.
##
## HumanModel:
##   A Meter is represented as a fixed length unit derived from the
##   fixed speed of light in vacuum and the defined duration of one
##   Second.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The meter is the length of the path traveled by light in vacuum
##   during 1/299,792,458 of a second.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   MeasurementType:
##     Length
##
##   ReferenceSystem:
##     International System of Units (SI)
##
##   ReferenceMedium:
##     Vacuum
##
##   ReferenceEntity:
##     Light
##
##   ReferenceSpeed:
##     SpeedOfLight
##
##   ReferenceDuration:
##     1/299,792,458 second
##
##   Definition:
##     One meter corresponds to the distance traveled by light in
##     vacuum during the specified fraction of one second.
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on independently observable and
##     measurable evidence of the defined speed of light in vacuum
##     and the defined temporal interval.
##
##   ReferenceIntegrity:
##     The reference system, reference medium, reference speed, and
##     reference duration used to determine the Meter must be
##     established independently of the length measurement being
##     verified.
##
##   MeasurementIntegrity:
##     Evidence used to determine the Meter must not be altered by
##     the entity or process whose length measurement is being verified.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine or realize the Meter must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine the Meter from the available evidence, reference
##     definitions, and assumptions.
##
##   MeasurementConfidence:
##     When the realization of the Meter cannot be established
##     directly, the verification process may provide a probability
##     representing the estimated likelihood that the measured or
##     realized length corresponds to the defined Meter.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the realization or measurement of the
##     Meter must be explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeMeasurements:
##     Physically plausible alternative interpretations of the
##     available optical, spatial, or temporal evidence must be
##     identified and considered where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableMeasurements:
##     If two or more physically distinct length states or measurement
##     conditions produce no physically observable distinction within
##     the available evidence, verification must not represent the
##     Meter realization as certain solely because one interpretation
##     is preferred by the inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish the realization or
##     measurement of the Meter with sufficient justification,
##     verification must report the unresolved measurement rather than
##     represent the value as established.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

#! Second - Unit of Time
## Symbol: s
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A base unit of time measurement defined by the fixed numerical
##   value of the cesium frequency, the unperturbed ground-state
##   hyperfine transition frequency of the cesium-133 atom.
##
## HumanModel:
##   A Second is represented as a fixed interval of time established
##   through a physical atomic transition frequency and used as the
##   fundamental unit for measuring elapsed time.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The second is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the
##   cesium frequency, the unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition
##   frequency of the cesium-133 atom, to be 9,192,631,770 when expressed
##   in the unit Hz.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   MeasurementType:
##     Time
##
##   ReferenceSystem:
##     International System of Units (SI)
##
##   ReferenceAtom:
##     Cesium-133
##
##   ReferenceTransition:
##     Unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency
##
##   Frequency:
##     9,192,631,770 Hz
##
##   Definition:
##     One second corresponds to the duration of
##     9,192,631,770 cycles of the specified cesium-133 transition.
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on independently observable and
##     measurable evidence of the defined cesium-133 reference
##     transition and its relationship to the defined time interval.
##
##   ReferenceIntegrity:
##     The reference system, reference atom, reference transition,
##     and fixed frequency value used to determine the Second must be
##     established independently of the time measurement being verified.
##
##   MeasurementIntegrity:
##     Evidence used to determine the Second must not be altered by
##     the entity or process whose temporal measurement is being verified.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine or realize the Second must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine the Second from the available evidence, reference
##     definitions, and assumptions.
##
##   MeasurementConfidence:
##     When the realization of the Second cannot be established
##     directly, the verification process may provide a probability
##     representing the estimated likelihood that the measured or
##     realized interval corresponds to the defined Second.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the realization or measurement of the
##     Second must be explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeMeasurements:
##     Physically plausible alternative interpretations of the
##     available atomic-frequency or temporal evidence must be
##     identified and considered where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableMeasurements:
##     If two or more physically distinct temporal states or
##     measurement conditions produce no physically observable
##     distinction within the available evidence, verification must
##     not represent the realization of the Second as certain solely
##     because one interpretation is preferred by the inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish the realization or
##     measurement of the Second with sufficient justification,
##     verification must report the unresolved measurement rather than
##     represent the value as established.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

#! Light Year - Distance Measurement
## Symbol: ly
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A unit of distance measurement representing the distance a beam
##   of light travels through vacuum during one Earth year.
##
## HumanModel:
##   A Light Year is represented as a fixed astronomical distance unit
##   derived from the speed of light in vacuum and the defined duration
##   of one Earth year.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   A light-year is the distance light travels through vacuum during
##   one Earth year. The resulting distance is approximately
##   9.46073 x 10^12 km (approximately 6 trillion miles).
##
## ExpectedState:
##   MeasurementType:
##     Distance
##
##   ReferenceMedium:
##     Light propagation through vacuum
##
##   ReferenceDuration:
##     One Earth year
##
##   ReferenceVelocity:
##     Speed of light in vacuum
##
##   Distance:
##     9.46073 x 10^12 km
##
##   EquivalentDistance:
##     Approximately 6 trillion miles
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on independently established
##     physical and mathematical definitions of the speed of light
##     in vacuum and the reference duration defining one Earth year.
##
##   ReferenceIntegrity:
##     The reference velocity, reference duration, and reference
##     conditions used to determine the Light Year must be established
##     independently of the distance value being verified.
##
##   MeasurementIntegrity:
##     The evidence used to determine the Light Year must not be
##     altered by the entity or process whose measurement is being
##     verified.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine the Light Year must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine the Light Year from the available evidence,
##     definitions, and assumptions.
##
##   MeasurementConfidence:
##     When the Light Year value cannot be established directly,
##     the verification process may provide a probability representing
##     the estimated likelihood that the measured or calculated value
##     is correct.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the Light Year determination must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeMeasurements:
##     Physically plausible alternative interpretations of the
##     available physical or temporal evidence must be identified and
##     considered where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableMeasurements:
##     If two or more physically distinct interpretations produce no
##     physically observable distinction within the available
##     evidence, verification must not represent the Light Year value
##     as certain solely because one interpretation is preferred by
##     the inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish the Light Year value
##     with sufficient justification, verification must report the
##     unresolved measurement rather than represent the value as
##     established.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

#! Epoch Time - Unit of Time Measurement
## Symbol: et
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A unit of time measurement representing the total number of
##   non-leap seconds elapsed since the Unix epoch at 00:00:00 UTC
##   January 1, 1970.
##
## HumanModel:
##   Epoch Time is represented as a numerical measurement of elapsed
##   time relative to a fixed temporal reference point.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The total number of non-leap seconds having elapsed since the
##   Unix epoch on 00:00:00 UTC January 1, 1970.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   ReferencePoint:
##     UnixEpoch
##
##   ReferenceTime:
##     00:00:00 UTC January 1, 1970
##
##   Measurement:
##     NonLeapSecondsElapsed
##
##   Value:
##     Integer count of elapsed seconds
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on independently measurable
##     temporal evidence relative to the defined reference point.
##
##   ReferenceIntegrity:
##     The reference point and reference time used for verification
##     must be established independently of the value being verified.
##
##   MeasurementIntegrity:
##     The evidence used to determine Epoch Time must not be altered
##     by the entity or process whose temporal state is being measured.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine the Epoch Time value must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine the Epoch Time value from the available evidence
##     and assumptions.
##
##   MeasurementConfidence:
##     When the exact Epoch Time value cannot be established directly,
##     the verification process may provide a probability representing
##     the estimated likelihood that the measured value is correct.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the Epoch Time measurement must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeMeasurements:
##     Physically plausible alternative interpretations of the
##     available temporal evidence must be identified and considered
##     where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableMeasurements:
##     If two or more physically distinct temporal states produce no
##     physically observable distinction within the available
##     evidence, verification must not represent the Epoch Time value
##     as certain solely because one interpretation is preferred by
##     the inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish the Epoch Time value
##     with sufficient justification, verification must report the
##     unresolved measurement rather than represent the value as
##     established.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

#! Galactic Center of Milky Way - Location
## Symbol: gc_milky_way
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A physical reference location representing the rotational center
##   of the Milky Way galaxy and associated with Sagittarius A*.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The Galactic Center is represented as a Location entity with
##   measurable astronomical properties.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The Galactic Center contains a supermassive black hole called
##   Sagittarius A*. Its mass is approximately 4 million solar masses
##   and its distance from Earth is approximately 8 kiloparsecs
##   (26000 light years) away from Earth at Galactic Coordinates
##   17h 45m 40.0409s, -29 degrees 00' 28.118.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   Object: Sagittarius A*
##   Mass: 4.0 million solar masses
##   DistanceFromEarth: 26000 light years
##   GalacticCoordinates: 17h 45m 40.0409s, -29 degrees 00' 28.118
##
## VerificationRequirements:
##   Evidence:
##     Verification must be based on physically observable and
##     independently measurable evidence.
##
##   IntendedSubject:
##     The verification process must evaluate whether the observed
##     phenomenon originated from the intended physical subject.
##
##   AlternativeSource:
##     The verification process must consider physically plausible
##     alternative sources or mechanisms capable of producing the
##     observed phenomenon.
##
##   Attribution:
##     Verification must distinguish measurement of an observed
##     phenomenon from attribution of that phenomenon to the
##     IntendedSubject.
##
##   EvidenceIntegrity:
##     Evidence used for attribution must be independently observable
##     and must not depend solely on claims made by the entity
##     being verified.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine attribution must be explicitly
##     identified.
##
##     The entity being verified must not be the sole authority for
##     establishing assumptions that determine its own attribution.
##
##   Inference:
##     The verification process must provide the reasoning basis used
##     to determine attribution from the available evidence,
##     hypotheses, and assumptions.
##
##   AttributionProbability:
##     When definitive attribution cannot be established, the
##     verification process may provide a probability representing
##     the estimated likelihood that the observed phenomenon
##     originated from the IntendedSubject rather than an
##     AlternativeSource.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the attribution assessment must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeExplanations:
##     Physically plausible alternative explanations capable of
##     producing the observed evidence must be identified and
##     considered.
##
##   IndistinguishableScenarios:
##     If two or more physically distinct scenarios produce no
##     physically observable distinction within the available
##     evidence, verification must not represent attribution as
##     certain solely because one scenario is preferred by the
##     inference model.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the available evidence cannot establish attribution with
##     sufficient justification, verification must report the
##     unresolved attribution rather than represent the attribution
##     as established.
##
## Authorization:
##   Verification provides evidence and attribution assessment.
##   Verification does not itself authorize physical displacement.
##
##   The Observer is the final Authorizer for temporal displacement.
##
## Precision:
##   exact
##
## VerificationTolerance:
##   0.00%
!#

->lib:sin:default:Time
->lib:sin:default:Location
->lib:sin:external:USF
->lib:sin:external:Observer
->lib:sin:external:TemporalDisplacement

framework:USF;

#! TemporalDisplacement - Physical Displacement Mechanism
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A mechanism representing one indivisible physical displacement
##   of an Observer from a source state to a destination state.
##
## HumanModel:
##   TemporalDisplacement is represented as a transition between two
##   complete Observer states. The transition itself is not an observable
##   state and does not expose any intermediate Observer condition.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   TemporalDisplacement represents one indivisible physical displacement.
##   From the Observer's perspective there is no observable intermediate
##   Observer state during the displacement. The Observer is either at the
##   source state or, if the displacement succeeds, at the destination state.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   SourceState:
##     The complete Observer state prior to displacement.
##
##   DestinationState:
##     The complete Observer state after successful displacement.
##
##   IntermediateState:
##     Does not exist as an observable Observer state.
##
##   TemporalDirection:
##     target_et may be earlier or later than source_et.
##     Negative temporal displacement is valid under the physical premise
##     of this project.
##
##   SuccessCondition:
##     Observer state equals destination state.
##
## Verification:
##   Verification must establish whether the proposed destination state
##   satisfies the requirements for physical displacement.
##
##   Verification provides evidence, assumptions, inference, uncertainty,
##   and attribution or measurement assessments where applicable.
##
##   Verification does not itself authorize physical displacement.
##
## Authorization:
##   The Observer is the final Authorizer for temporal displacement.
##
##   Physical displacement must not occur unless the Observer authorizes
##   the proposed transition.
##
## FailureCondition:
##   If verification cannot establish sufficient justification for the
##   proposed displacement, the Observer must not authorize the transition.
##
## HumanSemanticSummary:
##   This definition communicates that temporal displacement is an
##   indivisible physical transition between complete Observer states.
##   The Observer must move from its source state directly to its
##   destination state without an observable intermediate state.
##
##   Verification determines whether the proposed displacement is
##   sufficiently justified, but does not authorize or execute it.
##   The Observer remains the final Authorizer, and displacement must
##   not occur when verification is insufficient or authorization is absent.
!#
TemporalDisplacement:TemporalDisplacement;

self:Observer;
self.state(
    reference_entity=gc_milky_way,
    et=Time.now.et,
    x=Location.4d.x(Location.here.galactic_coordinates),
    y=Location.4d.y(Location.here.galactic_coordinates),
    z=Location.4d.z(Location.here.galactic_coordinates)
);
self.set_home_checkpoint();
self.set_recovery_checkpoint();

#! Build Observer Destination State
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A process responsible for constructing the complete destination
##   state of an Observer prior to execution of a temporal displacement.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The destination of a temporal displacement is represented as a
##   complete Observer state rather than as a sequence of individual
##   coordinate mutations.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   The destination is represented as a state rather than as a sequence
##   of coordinate mutations. The displacement operation is responsible
##   for the atomic transition from the current state to this destination
##   state.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   DestinationState:
##     Represents the complete Observer state after successful
##     displacement.
##
##   StateConstruction:
##     The destination state is constructed before the displacement
##     operation performs the transition.
##
##   StateCompleteness:
##     The destination state must contain all state information required
##     to represent the complete Observer state at the destination.
##
##   TransitionModel:
##     The displacement operation performs an atomic transition from
##     the current Observer state to the destination state.
##
##   MutationModel:
##     Coordinate-by-coordinate mutation is not used as the displacement
##     mechanism.
##
##   Verification:
##     Construction of a destination state does not establish that the
##     destination state is valid for temporal displacement.
##
##     The proposed destination state must be evaluated by the
##     verification process before displacement is authorized.
##
##   Authorization:
##     Destination state construction does not authorize temporal
##     displacement.
##
##     The Observer is the final Authorizer for temporal displacement.
##
##   VerificationFailure:
##     If the proposed destination state cannot be established as valid
##     with sufficient justification, the Observer must not authorize
##     displacement to that state.
##
## ErrorHandling:
##   Errors encountered while constructing the destination state must
##   be passed to framework.HandleError(
##     problem_code,
##     problem_sub,
##     problem_description
##   ).
##
##   Error handling must not authorize temporal displacement.
##
##   If destination state construction fails, the destination state must
##   not be represented as successfully constructed or valid for
##   displacement.
##
## HumanSemanticSummary:
##   This definition communicates that a temporal displacement destination
##   is a complete Observer state constructed before displacement rather
##   than a sequence of coordinate mutations.
##
##   Constructing a destination state does not establish that the state
##   is physically valid or authorize displacement. The destination must
##   be evaluated through verification, after which the Observer remains
##   the final Authorizer of the atomic transition.
!#
destination_state(
    observer,
    target_et,
    target_x,
    target_y,
    target_z
):function {
    ~
       ret observer.state(
          observer.reference_entity,
          target_et,
          target_x,
          target_y,
          target_z
       );
    ~-> err
       framework.HandleError(
          0,
          "destination_state",
          ("Observer destination state construction failed: " + err.msg)
       );
       ret 1, err;
    }
}

#! Validate Proposed Observer Displacement
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A validation process that evaluates a proposed temporal
##   displacement before execution without modifying the Observer state.
##
## HumanModel:
##   A proposed displacement is represented as a possible transition
##   between an Observer's current source state and a destination state.
##   Validation determines whether the proposed transition satisfies the
##   requirements for displacement before the transition occurs.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   Validation must not mutate Observer state. If validation fails,
##   the Observer remains exactly at its source state.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   ValidationScope:
##     The proposed displacement is evaluated without executing the
##     displacement operation.
##
##   MutationBehavior:
##     No Observer state changes occur during validation.
##
##   Evidence:
##     Validation must evaluate the available evidence relevant to the
##     proposed source state, destination state, and physical
##     displacement requirements.
##
##   Assumptions:
##     Assumptions used to determine whether the proposed displacement
##     is valid must be explicitly identified.
##
##   Inference:
##     The validation process must provide the reasoning basis used to
##     determine whether the proposed displacement satisfies the
##     required conditions.
##
##   Uncertainty:
##     Uncertainty affecting the validation assessment must be
##     explicitly identified.
##
##   AlternativeExplanations:
##     Physically plausible alternative explanations capable of
##     affecting the validation assessment must be identified and
##     considered where applicable.
##
##   IndistinguishableStates:
##     If two or more physically distinct states or conditions produce
##     no physically observable distinction within the available
##     evidence, validation must not represent one interpretation as
##     certain solely because it is preferred by the inference model.
##
##   FailureCondition:
##     If validation cannot establish that the proposed displacement
##     satisfies the required conditions with sufficient justification,
##     the Observer remains unchanged at the source state.
##
##   SuccessCondition:
##     A proposed displacement that satisfies the required conditions
##     may proceed to the authorization phase.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   The Observer source state before validation must be identical to
##   the Observer state after validation.
##
## Authorization:
##   Validation provides evidence and an assessment of whether the
##   proposed displacement satisfies the required conditions.
##
##   Validation does not authorize physical displacement.
##
##   The Observer is the final Authorizer for temporal displacement.
##
## AuthorizationFailure:
##   If the Observer does not authorize the proposed displacement,
##   the displacement must not occur regardless of the validation
##   assessment.
##
## ErrorHandling:
##   Errors encountered while performing displacement validation must
##   be passed to framework.HandleError(
##     problem_code,
##     problem_sub,
##     problem_description
##   ).
##
##   Error handling must not authorize temporal displacement.
##
##   A validation error must not be represented as successful validation.
##   The Observer must remain at the source state when validation cannot
##   be completed.
##
## HumanSemanticSummary:
##   This definition communicates that proposed displacement is evaluated
##   as a possible transition between complete source and destination
##   states without changing the Observer's current state.
##
##   Validation evaluates evidence, assumptions, inference, uncertainty,
##   and alternative explanations relevant to the proposed transition.
##   Validation provides an assessment but does not authorize execution;
##   the Observer is the final Authorizer, and failed or unresolved
##   validation leaves the Observer at its source state.
!#
validate_proposed_displacement(
    observer,
    target_state
):function {
    ~
       TemporalDisplacement.validate(observer, target_state);
    ~-> err
       framework.HandleError(
          0,
          "validate_proposed_displacement",
          ("Observer displacement validation failed: " + err.msg)
       );
       ret 1, err;
    }
    ret 0;
}

#! Execute Atomic Observer Displacement
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An operation that atomically transitions an Observer from its
##   complete source state to its complete destination state.
##
## HumanModel:
##   Temporal displacement is represented as a state transition between
##   two complete Observer states. The operation does not expose or
##   represent intermediate states during the transition.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   Atomically displace the Observer to a complete destination state.
##
##   Atomicity invariant:
##     success -> Observer is at destination_state
##     failure -> Observer remains at source_state
##
##   No intermediate Observer state is exposed by this operation.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   SourceState:
##     The complete Observer state before displacement execution.
##
##   DestinationState:
##     The complete Observer state after successful displacement.
##
##   Authorization:
##     Execution must occur only after the Observer has authorized the
##     proposed displacement.
##
##     Authorization must be based on the completed validation and
##     verification assessment for the proposed displacement.
##
##     This operation does not independently authorize displacement.
##
##   SuccessCondition:
##     Observer state equals destination_state.
##
##   FailureCondition:
##     Observer state remains identical to source_state.
##
##   TransitionVisibility:
##     Intermediate Observer states are not observable.
##
##   Atomicity:
##     The complete Observer state must transition from source_state
##     to destination_state as one indivisible physical operation.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   The operation must preserve Observer state integrity by ensuring
##   that failed displacement does not alter the source state.
##
##   A successful displacement must result in the complete Observer
##   state equaling destination_state.
##
## Verification:
##   Verification and validation provide the evidence and assessment
##   required for the Observer to determine whether the proposed
##   displacement satisfies the required conditions.
##
##   Verification does not itself authorize or execute the displacement.
##
## AuthorizationFailure:
##   If the Observer has not authorized the proposed displacement,
##   execution must not occur and the Observer must remain at
##   source_state.
##
## ErrorHandling:
##   Any error during destination state construction, validation,
##   authorization, or atomic displacement execution must be reported
##   to the caller and must not be treated as successful displacement.
##
##   Observer state must remain unchanged when displacement fails.
##
## HumanSemanticSummary:
##   This definition communicates that execution is the physical operation
##   that performs the authorized transition from one complete Observer
##   state to another as a single indivisible operation.
##
##   Successful execution means the Observer equals the destination state;
##   failed execution means the Observer remains at the source state.
##   No intermediate Observer state is exposed. Verification and validation
##   provide the supporting assessment, while the Observer's authorization
##   is required before execution and is not performed by this operation.
!#
move_observer(
	observer,
	target_et,
	target_x,
	target_y,
	target_z
):function {
	~
		target_state = destination_state(
			observer,
			target_et,
			target_x,
			target_y,
			target_z
		);
		validate_proposed_displacement(observer, target_state);
		TemporalDisplacement.displace(observer, target_state);
	~-> err
		framework.HandleError(
			0,
			"move_observer",
			("Atomic Observer displacement failed: " + err.msg)
		);
		ret 1, err;
	}
	framework.Log("Observer displaced atomically to destination state.");
	ret 0;
}

#! Recover Observer State Through Atomic Displacement
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An operation that restores an Observer to a previously defined
##   checkpoint state by performing a new atomic displacement from the
##   current Observer state.
##
## HumanModel:
##   Recovery is represented as an independent temporal displacement
##   operation. It is not a reversal, undo, or rollback of a previous
##   displacement event.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   Recover the Observer by performing another atomic displacement.
##
##   Recovery is not rollback of an interrupted movement. Because a
##   physical displacement is atomic from the Observer's perspective,
##   recovery is a separate atomic displacement from the current state
##   to the checkpoint.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   SourceState:
##     The Observer's complete current state at the time recovery
##     begins.
##
##   DestinationState:
##     The previously defined checkpoint state.
##
##   CheckpointIntegrity:
##     The checkpoint must represent a complete Observer state and must
##     be evaluated for validity before recovery is authorized.
##
##   RecoveryVerification:
##     Verification must evaluate whether the checkpoint remains a valid
##     destination for atomic displacement.
##
##     Verification must provide the evidence, assumptions, inference,
##     and uncertainty relevant to the validity of the checkpoint.
##
##   RecoveryMechanism:
##     Recovery occurs through the same atomic displacement semantics
##     as any other Observer displacement.
##
##   Authorization:
##     Recovery does not receive automatic authorization solely because
##     the checkpoint was previously defined.
##
##     The Observer is the final Authorizer for the recovery displacement.
##
##     The recovery displacement must not execute unless the Observer
##     authorizes the transition.
##
##   SuccessCondition:
##     Observer state equals the checkpoint state after successful
##     displacement.
##
##   FailureCondition:
##     Observer remains at the current source state.
##
##   TransitionVisibility:
##     No intermediate Observer state is exposed during recovery.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   Recovery does not modify or reverse a prior displacement event.
##   The current Observer state is the source state for the recovery
##   displacement.
##
##   A failed recovery must leave the Observer exactly at the source
##   state from which recovery was attempted.
##
## VerificationFailure:
##   If the checkpoint cannot be established as a valid destination
##   with sufficient justification, recovery must not be authorized.
##
## AuthorizationFailure:
##   If the Observer does not authorize the recovery displacement,
##   the recovery operation must not occur.
##
## ErrorHandling:
##   Any error during checkpoint retrieval, displacement validation,
##   authorization, or atomic recovery displacement must be reported
##   to the caller and must not be treated as successful recovery.
##
##   Observer state must remain unchanged when recovery fails.
##
## HumanSemanticSummary:
##   This definition communicates that recovery is a new atomic temporal
##   displacement from the Observer's current state to a previously
##   defined checkpoint state. Recovery is not rollback, undo, or reversal
##   of the preceding displacement.
##
##   The checkpoint must be treated as a proposed destination and
##   independently evaluated for validity before recovery. Verification
##   provides the assessment, but does not authorize recovery; the Observer
##   remains the final Authorizer, and failed recovery leaves the current
##   source state unchanged.
!#
recover_observer(observer):function {
	~
		checkpoint = observer.get_recovery_checkpoint();
		validate_proposed_displacement(observer, checkpoint);
		TemporalDisplacement.displace(observer, checkpoint);
	~-> err
		framework.HandleError(
			0,
			"recover_observer",
			("Atomic recovery displacement failed: " + err.msg)
		);
		ret 1, err;
	}
	framework.Log("Observer recovered by atomic displacement.");
	ret 0;
}

main():function {
	~
	    validate_current_state_result = TemporalDisplacement.validate_current_state(self);
        if (validate_current_state_result.valid == false) {
            throw (validate_current_state_result.failure_reason + ":" + validate_current_state_result.verification_details)
        }
	~-> err {
		framework.HandleError(
			0,
			"main",
			("Failed to verify initial Observer state: " + err.msg)
		);
		ret 1, err;
	}

    # Move one day into the past.
    # Negative temporal displacement is valid under the physical premise of this project.
    # target_et may be earlier than source_et.
	move_observer(
		self,
		(self.state.et - 86400),
		self.state.x,
		self.state.y,
		self.state.z
	);

    # Give observer three hours to look around
	wait(hours,3);

    # Move observer to recovery checkpoint
	recover_observer(self);

    # Let observer rest ten minutes
	wait(minutes,10);

	# Move one day into the future.
	move_observer(
		self,
		(self.state.et + 86400),
		self.state.x,
		self.state.y,
		self.state.z
	);

    # Give observer three hours to look around
	wait(hours,3);

    # Move observer to recovery checkpoint
	recover_observer(self);
	
	ret 0;
}
